  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 401 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nA northwest flow pattern persists over the Northern Plains \nearly this morning between a Hudson Bay low and Desert \nSouthwest high. A series of mid level shortwaves embedded in \nthe northwest flow are moving through central and southern \nSaskatchewan, where a few isolated showers and an occasional \nthunderstorm have been roaming around since earlier in the \nnight. More recently, a couple of showers have developed in \nnorthwest North Dakota along a tangential corridor of low level \nconvergence/warm air advection. These forcing mechanisms, along\nwith a surface trough that is deepening from western \nSaskatchewan through north central Montana, are forecast to \nslowly move southeastward through the day, reaching central \nNorth Dakota this evening. Model guidance is inconsistent on \nthe maintenance of ongoing activity through the morning, but \nthink it is likely that at least a few showers and perhaps an \noccasional thunderstorm will persist in northwest and north \ncentral North Dakota. Later in the day, CAMs project that \ndiurnal heating will contribute to an areal expansion of \nisolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms across \ncentral and southwest North Dakota. The probability for severe \nstorms in south central North Dakota later this afternoon and \nevening has greatly diminished as the surface trough is now \nforecast to be more diffuse and less progressive, resulting in \na weaker downstream moisture return that is now favored to \nlimit CAPE under 1000 J/kg, and with buoyancy decreasing over \ntime. A stronger storm or two still cannot be ruled out though \ngiven deep layer shear near 30 kts.\n\nToday is forecast to be warmer than yesterday, with highs \ngenerally around 80 northeast to near 90 southwest. Another \nshortwave that is over northern British Columbia early this \nmorning is forecast to reach the northern Red River Valley late\ntonight into Thursday morning. This may prolong isolated to \nscattered showers through the night from the Turtle Mountains \nthrough the James River Valley and points eastward. A cold \nfront attendant to this wave is also now forecast to drop \nthrough the state Thursday morning, which has caused a slight \ncooling of the high temperature forecast on Thursday to be more\nsimilar to that of today, rather than a few degrees warmer. On \nFriday, the upstream ridge is forecast to become highly \namplified over the Rockies. Deterministic guidance shows hints \nof a weak shortwave passage over the region on Friday, for \nwhich the NBM has introduced some low PoPs across northern \nNorth Dakota (10 to 30 percent). The high temperature forecast \non Friday remains similar across the south, but cools slightly \nin the north as a backdoor cold front tries to push down from \nCanada. Ensembles now favor Saturday to be cooler, albeit by \nonly around 5 degrees or less at any given location. There is \nstill uncertainty on the evolution of a potent shortwave \nlanding on the Pacific Northwest coast this weekend and how it \nwill interact with the stout upper ridge. Most solutions seem \nto favor the potent shortwave rounding the top of the ridge \nthrough the southern Canadian prairie provinces in the Sunday \nto Monday timeframe. The NBM still advertises a broad 20 to 30 \npercent chance of showers and thunderstorms late Saturday night \nthrough Sunday night, with future forecast refinement to be \ndetermined by the timing of the more potent shortwave moving \nacross southern Canada, as well as weaker shortwave energy that \nmay eject off the US Northern Rockies. The uncertainty in the \nevolution of the synoptic pattern keeps projected high \ntemperatures at near average values mostly in the 80s early \nnext week, but there does appear to be a slight favoring of the\nupper ridge rebuilding from the Southern Rockies through the \nNorthern High Plains.",
